  "nameStory": "Viereck's Sweat Bee gets its name from Henry Lorenz Viereck, an early American entomologist who specialized in studying wasps and bees in the early 1900s. The \"sweat bee\" part comes from these bees' habit of landing on people to sip the salt from human perspiration. It's one of many \"sand sweat bees\" that prefer sandy soils for their underground nests.",
  "description": "Viereck's Sweat Bee is a small, dark-colored native bee that's part of New England's important group of ground-nesting pollinators. Like other sweat bees, it's typically small enough to fit on your thumbnail, with a somewhat metallic sheen to its dark body. These solitary bees are excellent pollinators that dig their nests in sandy soil, where females provision underground chambers with pollen and nectar for their young. You'll often see them working late summer flowers, especially goldenrod, where their small size allows them to navigate between the tiny flower clusters.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Sandy habitat preference",
      "description": "Look for these bees in areas with sandy, well-drained soil where they can easily excavate their burrows. You might notice small holes in the ground nearby.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark metallic body",
      "description": "The body appears dark brown to black with a subtle metallic sheen that catches the light, giving it a slightly iridescent quality when viewed closely.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "About the size of a small ant or smaller than your pinky nail, much tinier than a honeybee but noticeably larger than the smallest gnats.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Goldenrod preference",
      "description": "Often seen hovering around or landing on goldenrod flowers in late summer, moving quickly between the small yellow flower clusters.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Quick, darting flight",
      "description": "Flies in quick, darting movements between flowers rather than the steady hovering of larger bees, with a slightly erratic pattern.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Viereck's Sweat Bee in sandy areas throughout New England, including coastal dunes, sandy riverbanks, and well-drained sandy soils in fields and forest edges. They're particularly common where sandy soil meets areas rich in late-summer wildflowers like goldenrod and other native plants. You'll often find them in the same areas where you see small holes in the sand from their nesting activities.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults are not typically active yet, though some may emerge from overwintering in late spring in warmer areas.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when adults emerge, mate, and females begin excavating nests in sandy soil while foraging on early summer flowers.",
    "fall": "Most visible time of year as they focus heavily on goldenrod and other late-blooming native flowers, building up resources for next year's generation.",
    "winter": "Inactive, overwintering as adults or late-stage larvae deep in their sandy burrows below the frost line."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called \"sweat bees,\" Viereck's Sweat Bee and its relatives are actually more interested in the salt in your perspiration than the sweat itself – they need the minerals for proper nutrition, making them nature's tiniest salt miners!",
  "confusionNotes": "With over 40 Lasioglossum species in New England, positive identification often requires expert examination. Focus on habitat (sandy areas) and flower preferences (especially goldenrod) rather than trying to distinguish between similar dark, small sweat bee species in the field.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The scientific name has changed several times – you might see this bee listed as Dialictus vierecki or Halictus vierecki in older field guides, which can be confusing for beginners trying to match names between different sources.",
  "compareHints": "Among the many Lasioglossum species in New England, Viereck's Sweat Bee stands out for its strong preference for sandy habitats and association with goldenrod. While species like the Deep-blue Sweat Bee have more obvious metallic coloring, Viereck's has a subtler dark metallic sheen. The habitat preference for sandy areas helps distinguish it from woodland species like the Leathery Sweat Bee that prefer different soil types.",
